{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"130\tthose turns under new event ids.\n131\t\n132\t## Risks\n133\t\n134\tNative formats change without notice (fabrication is version-pinned, bootstrap\n135\tis the recovery path); transcript content can include secrets (the ledger\n136\tredacts at capture and sync time, but matching is pattern/entropy-based and\n137\tcannot catch every secret — review before sharing still matters); full\n138\thistories can exceed the target's context (size is reported before launch);\n139\tcrashes mid-turn leave partial capture, which the ledger tolerates by design.\n140\t\n141\t## Roadmap\n142\t\n143\tToward an npm release (decided 2026-07-21; deepen the two existing targets\n144\tbefore broadening):\n145\t\n146\t- Publish `conversation-ledger` to npm (name is unclaimed), then swap the\n147\t  `file:` dependency for a semver range — `prepublishOnly` refuses to publish\n148\t  until this happens. Deliberately deferred while both packages are iterated\n149\t  on and dogfooded for a few days.\n150\t- Adapter-drift automation stays **reporting-only** (decided 2026-08-02). The\n151\t  daily check (`.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml`) opens a tracking issue on\n152\t  drift and now closes it when drift clears; it never writes to the repo.\n153\t  Auto-drafting the revalidation PR was built and then reverted. The reasoning\n154\t  is worth keeping, because the idea is tempting enough to recur: CI cannot\n155\t  validate a pin — the probes need both target CLIs installed, a TTY for the\n156\t  interactive smoke test, and paid model credentials per provider to make the\n157\t  recall turns real. So any PR CI opened would be a mechanical one-line bump\n158\t  with nothing behind it, saving a trivial edit while creating a *mergeable*\n159\t  artifact that asserts a validation nobody performed. An issue cannot be\n160\t  merged, and that asymmetry is the point. The agent-drafted variant (the old\n161\t  stage 2, needing an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret) fails for the same reason —\n162\t  the API key was never the binding constraint; the provider credentials the\n163\t  probes need are, and no amount of agency in CI conjures them.\n164\t- **opencode is a full member** *(target added 2026-08-02, opencode 1.18.5;\n165\t  capture landed in conversation-ledger 0.18.0 and verified here 2026-08-03)*.\n166\t  Both directions are exercised: a Claude Code conversation bridged into\n167\t  opencode is captured back by cledger's `session.idle` plugin, and bridging\n168\t  that opencode conversation onward into Codex and Claude Code preserves the\n169\t  tool call as a structured `function_call`/`function_call_output` pair and as\n170\t  `tool_use`/`tool_result` respectively, with the source model id intact.\n171\t  Note the asymmetry cledger's README calls out: opencode `reasoning` parts are\n172\t  plaintext, so they arrive as ordinary visible `thinking` blocks rather than\n173\t  as Codex-style opaque ciphertext — nothing here replays provider-private\n174\t  state for this target. Remaining opencode gaps: no headless model-recall\n175\t  probe (the Codex analogue is `probe-codex-content.mjs`), and long histories\n176\t  unmeasured — see the spec's \"Unknowns\".\n177\t- Revalidation tooling exists and is the bar for widening version pins:\n178\t  `npm run smoke:interactive` (real-TUI render check, all three targets),\n179\t  `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` (headless model-recall probe),\n180\t  `probe-session-invariants.mjs` (malformed-session tolerance),\n181\t  `probe-large-history.mjs` (long histories, `PROBE_TURNS=N`),\n182\t  `probe-codex-function-call.mjs` (structured tool replay), and\n183\t  `probe-picker.mjs` (pty-driven picker behavior).\n184\t- **Spec verification gaps** *(closed 2026-07-27, claude 2.1.220 /\n185\t  codex-cli 0.145.0)*. Every unknown the two spec docs listed has been\n186\t  probed and each is now marked RESOLVED in place 